#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="Gate: is this SHA green over the required check set? (0 green / 1 red / 2 could not look / 3 no answer yet)"
#
# ONE DEFINITION OF "IS THIS SHA GREEN". The predicate lived inside `ci-wait`'s
# poll loop, so the only way to ask it was to block until it answered. Anything
# that needed the answer once — `auto-release-land.yml` deciding whether to post
# `/fast-forward` — wrote its own, and the copy it wrote was weaker: it counted
# `select(.conclusion != "skipped")`, which reads a wholly skipped set as zero
# outstanding, i.e. green. That is CLOUD-327's false green with a second author.
# CLOUD-346 moved the predicate here; `ci-wait` polls around it, the workflow
# calls it once, and there is no second copy to drift.
#
# THE PREDICATE, unchanged from the one `ci-wait` carried: at least one required
# check graded, none skipped, none pending — scoped to $CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS
# (mise.toml [env]), never "any graded run". `SonarCloud Code Analysis` and
# `release-plz` are not draft-gated, so on a draft push the two of them grade on
# their own and satisfy "something graded" while every check that judges this
# repository is `skipped`. #261 landed on exactly that set.
#
# `skipped` is not a bad conclusion — it is the intended economy for a draft and
# for a matrix leg that does not run. What matters is WHERE it is read: a
# required check that skipped is not an answer (exit 3, and the caller decides
# whether to wait or stop); an unrelated check that skipped is none of our
# business. The same scoping is why a third party cannot veto a landing — only a
# required check's failure is red here, matching what branch protection enforces.
#
# `cancelled` sits in that same bucket, and CLOUD-363 is what put it there. A
# cancelled run judged nothing — it is the ABSENCE of an answer, not a bad one —
# and reading it as red wedged a branch with no exit: `land` re-drafted and
# stopped, and every later lap re-read the same stale set without ever being able
# to create a check-run. It holds for whatever cancels a run: a concurrent push
# under `cancel-in-progress`, a manual cancel, a runner reclaim. The bucket
# carries the conclusion alongside the name, because "no verdict" now has two
# spellings and a stall you cannot spell is a stall you cannot diagnose.
#
# ABSENT IS NOT SKIPPED, AND ABSENT IS NOT AN ANSWER EITHER (CLOUD-337). A
# path-filtered workflow produces no check-run at all, so `zizmor` on a PR
# touching no workflow is legitimately missing and requiring it would hang. That
# was implemented as an unconditional `continue` over every name with no run —
# and the unconditional half is a false green with #261's exact shape, one
# roster wide instead of one conclusion deep. On a fresh SHA `commit-lint`
# grades in seconds while `ci`, `cross`, `msrv`, `semver`, `darwin-link` and
# `final` have not registered, so a reading carrying that single success
# answered "every required check terminal and green" over a set of one. `land`
# then posted /fast-forward into a branch protection still listing the other six
# as expected, and the bot was rejected (measured on #280).
#
# So absence is legitimate for exactly the names $CI_ABSENT_OK_CHECKS lists
# (mise.toml [env] carries the two grounds each of them earns it on), and every
# other roster name must be PRESENT. Unset or empty is the STRICT direction,
# because the two failures are not symmetric: a name this gate waits for that
# never arrives is a stall — visible in the poll, self-naming, cleared by adding
# it there — while a name it forgives that had merely not registered yet is a
# landing nobody judged.
#
# ORDERING, and it is the subtle half: a real failure OUTRANKS a missing name,
# so the missing-name exit 3 fires only when the failed list is EMPTY. The
# asymmetry with the cancelled bucket below is deliberate and is argued where it
# is implemented, at the verdict.
#
# ONE NAME, ONE ANSWER: ITS LATEST RUN (CLOUD-436). A SHA accumulates a
# check-run per event, and a PR created as a draft mints a whole `skipped` set
# from its `opened` event. Ready it without pushing — `land`'s ordinary first
# lap whenever the branch was pushed before `gh pr create --draft` — and the
# graded set lands BESIDE those skips, on the same SHA, permanently. Judged as
# a union, the residue vetoes a verdict that already exists: measured three
# times in one evening, twice as an unbounded poll over a fully green head
# (#342, #345) and once, worse, as a poll blind to a completed `failure`
# (#343) — so the red that re-drafts a broken PR never fired.
#
# The skip is not wrong and is not ignored; it is SUPERSEDED, and only the
# newest run for a name speaks for it. `started_at` orders them — ISO-8601
# sorts lexicographically, so a string compare is chronological — with the run
# `id` breaking a tie inside one second. Measured on #345's residue: the skip
# at 03:18:10Z (id ...182343) against the success at 03:20:16Z (id ...484435),
# so either field alone discriminates and the two agree.
#
# The draft economy is untouched (CLOUD-247, CLOUD-327): a name whose LATEST
# run skipped is still not an answer. What changed is only that a skip some
# graded run has already replaced stops speaking for that name.
#
# Four outcomes, because the caller's next move differs for each: 0 green, 1 red
# (a required check failed — terminal, stop), 2 could not look (unset roster, an
# API that would not answer — the case where this gate decided nothing), 3 no
# answer yet (pending, a required check skipped, or a required name with no run
# at all — poll again, or hold). A
# reading is injectable through $CHECKS_GREEN_RUNS as TSV lines
# `status<TAB>conclusion<TAB>name<TAB>started_at<TAB>id`, which is both how
# `ci-wait` hands over the body it already fetched conditionally and how the
# tests run with no network. The last two fields are the ordering key. A
# reading that omits them still answers, and answers exactly as it did before
# latest-per-name existed: where two runs of one name cannot be ordered, the
# LEAST conclusive of them wins, so an unorderable pair can never read greener
# than the union it replaces.
#
# Output is pointer-only per non-negotiable rule 4: a `<conclusion> <check>`
# coordinate, never a run's log.
#
# MUTATION COVERAGE (CLOUD-418). `<slug>|<sed script>|<case name>`: applying
# the script to a throwaway copy of this file must turn the named case RED.
# A gate listed in $MUTANT_GATES with no row here fails `mise run mutant`.
#MUTANT answered-catch-all|s/!(\$2 in isanswer) ? 3/0 ? 3/|CLOUD-376: AN UNKNOWN CONCLUSION HOLDS THE POLL OPEN

set -uo pipefail

fail_input() {
	echo "::error:: checks-green: $*" >&2
	exit 2
}

# Declared once in mise.toml [env] and read by `ci-wait` and `land` too. Unset is
# fatal rather than an empty set: an empty set makes every check unrequired,
# which is the false green this gate exists to stop.
required="${CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS:-}"
[ -n "$required" ] ||
	fail_input "CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS is unset — run this through \`mise run checks-green\`, which is where the required set is declared."

# The names for which having NO run at all is a legitimate reading, read beside
# the roster and deliberately NOT fatal when unset (CLOUD-337). Unset is the
# empty set, which is the STRICT direction — every roster name must be present —
# and that asymmetry with $CI_REQUIRED_CHECKS above is the point: forgetting the
# roster makes every check unrequired, while forgetting this one only makes the
# gate wait for a name that may never come. A stall is loud, bounded by whoever
# reads the poll, and costs time; a false green lands a SHA nothing judged and is
# not recoverable after the fact.
absent_ok="${CI_ABSENT_OK_CHECKS:-}"

# The conclusions that constitute an ANSWER, declared once in mise.toml [env] and
# read by `land`'s `graded_runs` too (CLOUD-376). Fatal when unset for the same
# reason the roster is: an empty set makes every conclusion an answer, which is
# the false green in a new spelling.
answered="${CI_ANSWERED_CONCLUSIONS:-}"
[ -n "$answered" ] ||
	fail_input "CI_ANSWERED_CONCLUSIONS is unset — run this through \`mise run checks-green\`, which is where the answered set is declared."

sha="${SHA:-$(git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)}"

# `${VAR+set}` rather than emptiness: an explicitly empty reading is a real
# state — a SHA with no check-runs yet — and must answer "no answer yet" rather
# than fall through to the network.
if [ -n "${CHECKS_GREEN_RUNS+set}" ]; then
	runs="$CHECKS_GREEN_RUNS"
else
	[ -n "$sha" ] || fail_input "no SHA to judge: neither \$SHA nor a git HEAD."
	# `{owner}/{repo}` is gh's own placeholder, resolved from the checkout's
	# remote; $REPO overrides it where the caller already knows (a workflow).
	repo="${REPO:-}"
	[ -n "$repo" ] || repo='{owner}/{repo}'
	runs=$(gh api "repos/$repo/commits/$sha/check-runs?per_page=100" \
		--jq '.check_runs[]? | "\(.status)\t\(.conclusion // "-")\t\(.name)\t\(.started_at // "")\t\(.id // 0)"' 2>/dev/null) ||
		fail_input "could not read the check-runs for $sha. A reading this gate cannot take is not a pass."
fi

# One pass over the required subset. `|`-separated because a tab is IFS
# whitespace and `read` collapses runs of it, which would shift an empty name
# list into the wrong variable; no check-run name contains a pipe.
reading=$(printf '%s\n' "$runs" | awk -F'\t' -v req="$required" -v absent_ok="$absent_ok" -v answered="$answered" '
	BEGIN {
		n = split(req, roster, ","); for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) want[roster[i]] = 1
		# CLOUD-376: one declared set, not a literal repeated here and in `land`.
		m = split(answered, concls, ","); for (i = 1; i <= m; i++) isanswer[concls[i]] = 1
		# An unset or empty $CI_ABSENT_OK_CHECKS splits into zero fields, so the
		# tolerated set is empty and every roster name must be present. The
		# strict direction is reached by construction here rather than by a
		# guard above that a later edit could forget to keep (CLOUD-337).
		k = split(absent_ok, exempt, ","); for (i = 1; i <= k; i++) tolerated[exempt[i]] = 1
	}
	!NF || !($3 in want) { next }
	{
		name = $3
		# ISO-8601 sorts lexicographically, so a string compare is chronological;
		# the zero-padded id breaks a tie inside one second.
		key = $4 "|" sprintf("%020d", $5 + 0)
		# The precedence that makes an UNORDERABLE pair fail closed: a reading
		# carrying no started_at/id leaves every key equal, and then the least
		# conclusive row wins, which is the union answer this replaces.
		# `cancelled` ranks with `skipped`, not with a failure (CLOUD-363): a
		# cancelled run judged nothing, so an unorderable pair holding one must
		# fall to "no answer" rather than to red.
		rank = ($1 != "completed") ? 4 : \
			(!($2 in isanswer) ? 3 : (($2 == "success" || $2 == "neutral") ? 1 : 2))
		if (!(name in bestkey) || key > bestkey[name] ||
			(key == bestkey[name] && rank > bestrank[name])) {
			bestkey[name] = key
			bestrank[name] = rank
			beststatus[name] = $1
			bestconcl[name] = $2
		}
	}
	END {
		# Iterated in roster order rather than `for (name in ...)`, whose order
		# awk does not define — this output is a contract (house style §6).
		for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
			name = roster[i]
			# NO RUN AT ALL FOR THIS NAME. This was an unconditional `continue`,
			# which made a name with no run invisible and answered the whole
			# roster from whichever check happened to grade first (CLOUD-337).
			# Tolerated only for the names the caller declared absent-ok, whose
			# workflow is paths-filtered AND takes the default event types, so
			# it never fires on ready_for_review; every other absence is a name
			# that has not registered yet, which is a fresh SHA, not an answer.
			if (!(name in bestkey)) {
				if (name in tolerated) continue
				missing = missing sep2 name
				sep2 = ", "
				continue
			}
			print "SUMMARY|" bestconcl[name] "\t" name
			if (beststatus[name] != "completed") { pending++; continue }
			# THE NOT-AN-ANSWER BUCKET, and `cancelled` belongs in it (CLOUD-363).
			# A cancelled run is the ABSENCE of a verdict, exactly like a
			# draft-era skip; reading it as red wedged #293 with no exit. The
			# conclusion is carried beside the name because "no verdict" has
			# several spellings, and a stall you cannot spell is a stall you
			# cannot diagnose.
			#
			# MEMBERSHIP, NOT A LITERAL PAIR (CLOUD-376). The old test named
			# `skipped` and `cancelled` and let everything else fall through to
			# red — so a conclusion GitHub adds tomorrow, which nobody has ever
			# seen, would be reported as a verdict against a head it never
			# judged. Not in the answered set now means "no answer", and the poll
			# continues: recoverable, where a false red costs a re-draft and a lap.
			if (!(bestconcl[name] in isanswer)) {
				skipped = skipped sep3 name " " bestconcl[name]
				sep3 = ", "
				continue
			}
			graded++
			if (bestconcl[name] != "success" && bestconcl[name] != "neutral") {
				bad = bad sep4 name " " bestconcl[name]
				sep4 = ", "
			}
		}
		printf "VERDICT|%d|%d|%s|%s|%s\n", graded + 0, pending + 0, skipped, bad, missing
	}
')
# The judged view — one line per required name that has a run, carrying the very
# rows the verdict was taken over, so the summary can never contradict it.
summary=$(printf '%s\n' "$reading" | sed -n 's/^SUMMARY|//p')
verdict=$(printf '%s\n' "$reading" | sed -n 's/^VERDICT|//p')
IFS='|' read -r graded pending ungraded failed missing <<<"$verdict"

# "No answer" is tested BEFORE "red", and the order is load-bearing rather than
# incidental (CLOUD-363). The measured set on #293 was `final failure` plus five
# `cancelled`: `final` is a fan-in over the others, so its failure was a
# CONSEQUENCE of the cancellations and not an independent verdict on the tree.
# Promoting red over "no answer" would report that set as a real failure and put
# the branch straight back in the wedge. A genuine failure leaves this bucket
# empty and still falls through to exit 1 below.
if [ "$graded" -eq 0 ] || [ "$pending" -gt 0 ] || [ -n "$ungraded" ]; then
	if [ -n "$ungraded" ]; then
		echo "checks-green: not an answer yet on $sha — required check(s) with no verdict: $ungraded"
	else
		echo "checks-green: not an answer yet on $sha — $pending required check(s) still running, $graded graded"
	fi
	exit 3
fi

# A REQUIRED NAME WITH NO RUN AT ALL IS NOT AN ANSWER EITHER (CLOUD-337) — and
# unlike the bucket above, it YIELDS to a failure rather than outranking one.
# The `-z "$failed"` guard is the whole subtlety, and the asymmetry is about
# what each state can MANUFACTURE.
#
# A cancelled sibling can manufacture a fan-in failure: `final` declares
# `needs:` over the others, so cancelling them makes it fail without judging
# anything (#293's measured set), and reading that as red wedged the branch with
# no exit — which is why no-verdict precedes red up there.
#
# An absent name manufactures nothing. A check-run that was never created cannot
# have caused a completed `failure` beside it, so that failure is an independent
# verdict on this tree and must still exit 1 and re-draft the PR. Holding the
# poll open for the stragglers instead would leave a PR ready over a tree
# already known to be red, buying a runner per lap to re-learn it.
if [ -n "$missing" ] && [ -z "$failed" ]; then
	echo "checks-green: not an answer yet on $sha — required check(s) with no run at all: $missing"
	exit 3
fi

printf '%s\n' "$summary"
if [ -n "$failed" ]; then
	echo "::error:: CI is not green on $sha — $failed. Reproduce and fix locally." >&2
	exit 1
fi
echo "checks-green: every required check terminal and green on $sha"
